  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Colder air will be accompanied by scattered wet snow \nand rain showers through this weekend with minor snow accumulations \nexpected across the higher terrain.\n\nThe set-up will be favorable for light upslope flow-induced and lake-\neffect precipitation today through tonight generally south of the \neastern Great Lakes. However, a lack of synoptic moisture should \nprevent the formation of any organized precipitation. Cold air \nadvection and low atmospheric melting levels will support rain \nmixing with or changing to wet snow at times at lower elevations and \nprimarily wet snow at higher elevations. Very little or no snow \naccumulation is expected at lower elevations, but additional snow \naccumulations of a coating to 2\" are expected along the Chautauqua \nRidge and Bristol Hills.\n\nA sharp mid/upper-level trough axis is expected to approach and then \nswing south-southeastward through our region Saturday through \nSaturday night. This will support an uptick in precipitation across \nWestern and Central NY, however this uptick should be fairly short-\nlived. A quick coating of snow accumulation is possible at lower \nelevations, while an additional coating to 1\" of snow accumulation \nis expected at higher elevations of Western NY, the Finger Lakes \nRegion, and North-Central NY, including the Tug Hill Plateau.\n\nHigh pressure at the surface and aloft builds from the west on \nSunday through Sunday night, while subtle shortwave trough axes \nembedded in the northerly mid/upper-level flow traverse Western and \nCentral NY. Much drier air behind Saturday night's mid/upper-level \ntrough axis passage will expand through the dendritic snow growth \nzone Sunday morning, with any lingering upslope flow-induced and \nlake-induced wet snow and/or rain showers amidst north-northwesterly \nmean low-level flow ending through the morning hours. A few wet snow \nand/or rain showers may linger across Central NY into the early \nafternoon hours. The still cool northwesterly to north-northwesterly \nmean low-level flow and shallow low-level moisture on the synoptic-\nscale will maintain a wealth of upslope flow-induced and lake-effect \ncloud cover through the afternoon and overnight time period.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will return by Monday, with temperatures \ntrending warmer by midweek.\n\nAn expansive ridge at the surface and aloft will continue to build \nfrom the Great Plains and across the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley \nearly next week. This feature will bring a return to dry conditions, \nwith perhaps a few partly to mostly sunny days. Temperatures will \nremain cool ahead of the surface high, but as this surface high \nslides eastward, and a stronger southerly flow ensues, afternoon \ntemperatures will rise back into the lower 50s by Tuesday or \nWednesday of next week...with the warming trend to well above normal \ntemperatures continuing into Thursday.",
